// Runtime/CheckedDivisionRuntime.maxon — the EXPANDED DIVISION family (A1, A1x, X10): the five callee names
// a `/` or `mod` desugars to when the compiler owes it a guard, the error enum the fallible four throw, and
// the one ordinal they throw it as.
//
// ⚠ **"CHECKED" IS ABOUT THE GUARD, NOT ABOUT THROWING, and since A1x the family has a member that raises
// nothing.** `idiv` faults for TWO reasons: a zero divisor (catchable — A1) and an unrepresentable quotient
// (`i64.min / -1`). The second is fatal for `/` by design and IMPOSSIBLE for `mod`, whose remainder at that
// divisor is simply `0` — so A1x's `__guarded_mod` is expanded exactly as its siblings are and is TOTAL.
// `divisionKindThrows` is the one place that difference is decided.
//
// ⭐ **WHY A "RUNTIME" FILE THAT INSTALLS NO RUNTIME FUNCTION.** Every other throwing builtin family here
// is a set of BUILT Std functions (`__managed_get`, `__mf_open_read`, `__md_next`, `__gt_process_run`), and its
// file holds the builders. This one holds none, because a checked divide is EXPANDED IN PLACE at the point
// of use (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerDivisionExpansion`) rather than called: the whole body is a compare, the
// `setcc` that materializes it, an `or`, and the divide the program asked for — so a `call`/`ret` around it
// would be a function whose prologue costs more than its work, the same argument `emitMonotonicMs` makes for
// inlining its divide instead of building a `__clock_now_ms`. The bootstrap reaches the identical conclusion
// (`MaxonToStandardConversion.ManagedMemory.cs:2336-2394` lowers `__checked_div` inline and deliberately
// calls no runtime helper).
//
// What the file DOES own is everything a family needs BESIDES a body — and every one of those is a fact
// some other file has to agree with, which is exactly why they live together in one:
//
//   • the five CALLEE NAMES, and the two classifiers that map a name back to WHICH division it is and at
//     WHAT SIGNEDNESS;
//   • the THROWING predicate `isThrowingRuntimeCallee` composes (so `try` is accepted, and a bare divide
//     is refused with E3057);
//   • the `throws` CLAUSE `runtimeThrowsClause` answers with (so an `otherwise (e)` binding is typed
//     `__DivisionByZeroError` and `match e` dispatches on its cases rather than reporting a gap);
//   • the ORDINAL the lowering encodes, and the `BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck` claim that holds it to the
//     declaration.
//
// ⭐ **THE PREDICATE AND THE LOWERING'S DISPATCH ARE ONE LIST, DERIVED, NOT TWO LISTS CHECKED.** The other
// four families need a bidirectional install-time guard (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.verifyThrowingAccessorsMatchPredicate`)
// because a name-listing predicate and a set of builders that stamp a `throws` clause are genuinely two
// copies of one fact, and only a check can hold them together. Here there is nothing to check: the two name
// CONSTANTS below are the only place any name is WRITTEN, `divisionKindOf` and `divisionCalleeSignedness`
// are the only classifiers over them, `isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee` is DERIVED from `divisionKindThrows`, and `lowerDivisionExpansion`
// matches that same kind EXHAUSTIVELY — so a fourth kind cannot be named as throwing without the lowering
// refusing to compile, and cannot be expanded without being named. A guard over one list would be a check with nothing on
// the other side of it. (Ordinal drift is a different fact with a different pair of copies, and it DOES get
// its claim below.)
let CheckedDivideName = b"__checked_div"
let CheckedModuloName = b"__checked_mod"

// ⭐⭐ **THE UNSIGNED TWINS (X10), AND WHY THEY ARE NAMES RATHER THAN TWO MORE `CheckedDivisionKind`
// CASES.** A divide's SIGNEDNESS and its KIND are two independent facts about one operator, and
// crossing them here would have doubled a union that four exhaustive matches elsewhere are held to —
// each of which would then have had to answer a question it does not ask. So the KIND stays three
// cases (what is divided, and whether the compiler owes it a guard) and the signedness is a SECOND
// question over the same one list of names, answered by `divisionCalleeSignedness` below.
//
// The callee name has to carry it because the name is the ONLY channel that survives to the lowering:
// `lowerTryCall` reconstructs everything it knows about an expanded divide from the callee it was
// handed. `Parser.emitDivOrMod` decides the signedness (it is the last tier that can — see TypeRules'
// ⭐⭐ THE DIVISION SIGNEDNESS RULE), and this is where that decision is written down.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `__guarded_mod_unsigned`, AND THAT ABSENCE IS THE RULE WORKING.** The total
// remainder exists because `idiv` raises `#DE` on `i64.min mod -1`; read UNSIGNED those two patterns
// are `2^63 mod (2^64 - 1)` = `2^63`, an ordinary answer no hardware refuses (x64's `div` reads a
// ZEROED RDX, so no quotient can overflow; arm64's `udiv` and wasm's `i64.rem_u` have no overflow trap
// at all). So an unsigned `mod` has exactly ONE hazard — the zero divisor — and a divisor proven
// non-zero takes the BARE route, exactly as an unsigned `/` does. `Parser.emitDivOrMod` states the
// same fact from its end by never asking for the overflow guard on an unsigned remainder.
let UnsignedCheckedDivideName = b"__checked_div_unsigned"
let UnsignedCheckedModuloName = b"__checked_mod_unsigned"

// ⭐⭐ **THE THIRD EXPANSION, AND THE ONLY ONE THAT THROWS NOTHING (A1x).** `idiv` raises `#DE` for TWO
// unrelated reasons and A1 desugared only the first, so a `mod` whose divisor the compiler PROVED
// non-zero — a literal, or a ranged type excluding 0 — still went out as a bare `idiv` and still died on
// `i64.min mod -1`. **That case is TOTAL, not fallible**: `a mod -1` is `0` for every `a`, so there is
// nothing for a `try` to catch and requiring one would be a lie about the operation. Hence a callee of
// its own rather than a flag on `__checked_mod`: the throwing-ness of a division rides its NAME
// (`isThrowingRuntimeCallee`), and this one must answer `false` there while still being EXPANDED rather
// than called.
//
// ⚠ **`/` HAS NO COUNTERPART AND MUST NOT GROW ONE.** `i64.min / -1` has no representable quotient, so
// there is no value a total `/` could return; `specs-shv2/division.md` documents that fault as
// deliberate. The asymmetry is the whole reason the two operators part company here: `mod` tests only
// its DIVISOR, `/` would have to test both operands and would still have nothing to answer.
let GuardedModuloName = b"__guarded_mod"

// ⭐ **WHICH DIVISION A CHECKED-DIVISION CALLEE NAMES** — the one list, read three ways (the throwing
// predicate below, the E3057 message's wording, and the Std expansion's opcode choice). `notCheckedDivision`
// is the ordinary answer for every other callee in the program, so this is asked on the general call path
// and must not allocate: both arms return a payload-free variant.
// ⚠ **"CHECKED" HERE MEANS "THE COMPILER INSERTED A CHECK", NOT "IT CAN THROW"** — which is why A1x's
// total remainder is a member rather than a fourth thing beside this union. All three are expanded in
// place with a guard the source did not write; whether that guard has an ERROR CHANNEL is a separate
// fact, answered once by `isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee` below and by nothing else.
export union CheckedDivisionKind
	// `a / b` — the QUOTIENT. Integer (`StdOp.div`) or float (`binOp(div)`), decided by the operand type at
	// lowering, which is why one name serves both: the desugar is about FALLIBILITY, not about width.
	quotient
	// `a mod b` — the REMAINDER, over a divisor that might be ZERO. Integer only; float `mod` does not
	// exist in the language. Its expansion carries the `-1` guard as well, unconditionally — see
	// `lowerDivisionExpansion`.
	remainder
	// `a mod b` — the REMAINDER over a divisor PROVEN non-zero but possibly `-1` (A1x). TOTAL: guarded
	// against `idiv`'s overflow `#DE` and nothing else, so it has no error flag and no `throws` clause.
	//
	// ⚠ **APPENDED AFTER `remainder` DELIBERATELY.** `isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee`'s range arm reads
	// `quotient to remainder`, so a member inserted INSIDE that range would silently join the throwing
	// set — a total operation the parser would then demand a `try` on. At the end of the two throwing
	// kinds it falls outside every existing range arm instead, which makes each one a compile error until
	// it is answered on purpose.
	guardedRemainder
	notCheckedDivision
end 'CheckedDivisionKind'

export function divisionKindOf(callee ByteArray) returns CheckedDivisionKind
	if callee.equals(CheckedDivideName) or callee.equals(UnsignedCheckedDivideName) 'divide'
		return CheckedDivisionKind.quotient
	end 'divide'

	if callee.equals(CheckedModuloName) or callee.equals(UnsignedCheckedModuloName) 'modulo'
		return CheckedDivisionKind.remainder
	end 'modulo'

	if callee.equals(GuardedModuloName) 'guardedModulo'
		return CheckedDivisionKind.guardedRemainder
	end 'guardedModulo'

	return CheckedDivisionKind.notCheckedDivision
end 'divisionKindOf'

// ⭐ **THE OTHER QUESTION THE ONE NAME LIST ANSWERS (X10)** — beside `divisionKindOf`, not folded into
// it, because the KIND decides the error channel and the guard while this decides only the
// INSTRUCTION. Its single caller is `lowerDivisionExpansion`, which hands the answer straight to
// `divisionOpcodeFor` so that the expanded divide and the bare one pick their signedness through the
// same function.
//
// Every name that is not one of the two unsigned ones is `signed`, including `notCheckedDivision`'s
// non-division callees — this is asked only of a name already classified as an expanded division, and
// `signed` is what the whole language emitted before X10, so a name that ever fell through would fall
// through to the old behaviour rather than to a wrong instruction.
export function divisionCalleeSignedness(callee ByteArray) returns DivideSignedness
	if callee.equals(UnsignedCheckedDivideName) or callee.equals(UnsignedCheckedModuloName) 'unsigned'
		return DivideSignedness.unsigned
	end 'unsigned'

	return DivideSignedness.signed
end 'divisionCalleeSignedness'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS KIND READ A REMAINDER?** — the one place the two remainder kinds are treated as one. ONE
// caller today (`lowerDivisionExpansion`'s overflow-guard gate), so it is a named FACT rather than a saving:
// a union cannot be compared with `or`, so asking it inline would mean a second `match` over
// `CheckedDivisionKind` whose arms nothing holds to this one's. The QUOTIENT is excluded for the reason this
// whole rung turns on — `a / -1` overflows for exactly one dividend and has no answer when it does, while
// `a mod -1` is `0` for all of them — and stating that in a named predicate is what makes a future third
// remainder kind land on the right side of it by having to choose.
export function divisionKindReadsRemainder(kind CheckedDivisionKind) returns bool
	return match kind 'k'
		quotient gives false
		remainder to guardedRemainder gives true
		notCheckedDivision gives false
	end 'k'
end 'divisionKindReadsRemainder'

// The callee name a source-level `/` or `mod` desugars to — the operator → name direction, beside the
// name → kind direction above so the three constants are the ONE place any of them is written. Its gate is
// `TypeRules.divisionOperator`, and `Parser.emitDivOrMod` is its only caller: any other operator here is a
// routing bug in the expression climber, so it panics rather than defaulting to the quotient (which would
// silently turn `a + b` into a division).
//
// ⭐ **`fallible` IS THE ZERO VERDICT, AND IT IS THE ONLY THING THAT DECIDES BETWEEN THE TWO REMAINDERS**
// (A1x). A `mod` reaches this door for one of two unrelated reasons — its divisor might be 0, or its
// divisor might be `-1` — and the name has to say which, because the name is where `isThrowingRuntimeCallee`
// reads a division's error channel from. Both are expanded; only the first can throw.
//
// ⚠ A TOTAL `/` never reaches here at all: its only hazard IS the zero divisor, so a `/` the parser
// proved non-zero is emitted as a bare `idiv` and needs no expansion. That is a panic rather than a
// comment because the alternative — returning `CheckedDivideName` for it — would silently make an
// unguarded quotient look guarded.
export function divisionExpansionCalleeForOp(op MaxonBinOp, fallible bool) returns ByteArray
	if op == MaxonBinOp.div 'divide'
		requireFallibleQuotient(op, fallible: fallible)
		return CheckedDivideName
	end 'divide'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.mod 'modulo'
		return CheckedModuloName if fallible else GuardedModuloName
	end 'modulo'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.divUnsigned 'unsignedDivide'
		requireFallibleQuotient(op, fallible: fallible)
		return UnsignedCheckedDivideName
	end 'unsignedDivide'

	// An unsigned remainder has only the zero hazard (see `UnsignedCheckedModuloName`), so — unlike its
	// SIGNED twin — a divisor proven non-zero leaves it nothing to expand and it never reaches here.
	if op == MaxonBinOp.modUnsigned 'unsignedModulo'
		requireFallibleQuotient(op, fallible: fallible)
		return UnsignedCheckedModuloName
	end 'unsignedModulo'

	panic("divisionExpansionCalleeForOp: '{op.rawValue}' is not a division operator — `divisionOperator` is the gate and `emitDivOrMod` its only caller")
end 'divisionExpansionCalleeForOp'

// The three operators with NOTHING to expand once their divisor is proven non-zero, refused in one
// place rather than three: the two quotients, whose only hazard IS the zero divisor, and the unsigned
// remainder, which has no `-1` overflow to guard toward. Returning a checked name for any of them
// would silently make an unguarded divide look guarded.
function requireFallibleQuotient(op MaxonBinOp, fallible bool)
	if fallible 'hasAZeroHazard'
		return
	end 'hasAZeroHazard'

	panic("divisionExpansionCalleeForOp: a TOTAL `{op.rawValue}` has no expansion — its only hazard is a zero divisor, and the second hazard `__guarded_mod` exists for (`i64.min mod -1`) is either unanswerable (`i64.min / -1`, specs-shv2/division.md) or absent (an unsigned divide reads a ZEROED high half and cannot overflow). Only a SIGNED `mod` has a second, answerable hazard.")
end 'requireFallibleQuotient'

// The kind → OPERATOR direction, which closes the cycle the two functions above open (operator → name →
// kind) and is what lets `lowerDivisionExpansion` hand the divide back to `lowerBinOp` — the ONE place that
// decides what a `/` or a `mod` becomes — instead of spelling `StdOp.div` / `StdOp.mod` a second time.
//
// ⚠ It is an EXHAUSTIVE match, deliberately: a fourth member of `CheckedDivisionKind` stops this function
// compiling, which is the only thing that can stop a new checked division from being lowered as a quotient
// by omission. `notCheckedDivision` is not a division and has no operator, so it panics rather than
// defaulting to one — `lowerTryCall`'s own match is what keeps it unreachable.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS THE ONLY PLACE A KIND IS CLASSIFIED AS A QUOTIENT OR A REMAINDER**, which is why the
// DIAGNOSTIC spelling below is derived from its answer rather than matching on the kind a second time.
export function divisionOperatorForKind(kind CheckedDivisionKind) returns MaxonBinOp
	match kind 'k'
		quotient then return MaxonBinOp.div
		remainder to guardedRemainder then return MaxonBinOp.mod
		notCheckedDivision then panic("divisionOperatorForKind: `notCheckedDivision` names no operator — `lowerTryCall` and `lowerRuntimeCall` route only the three division kinds into the expansion")
	end 'k'
end 'divisionOperatorForKind'

// ⭐ **HOW A DIAGNOSTIC SPELLS A DIVISION OPERATOR** — one mapping, because three messages quote it and a
// divide reported as `mod` (or the reverse) would send the reader to the wrong line: E3057's "throwing
// division requires try" (`SemanticCheck`), E3055's "cannot fail" for the total remainder
// (`Parser.requireThrowingNamedTryTarget`), and E3103's provable-zero refusal — which reads it straight off
// the operator token (`opTok.kind.rawValue`).
//
// The spelling is `TokenKind`'s for exactly that reason: E3103 has a token in hand and the other two have
// only a callee, so `TokenKind` is the one source that keeps all three saying the same word. A `%` synonym
// for `mod` would move every one of them at once.
//
// It takes the OPERATOR and not the KIND, and that is the fix for a second copy of the classification: the
// kind → spelling form matched `quotient` / `remainder to guardedRemainder` a second time, so the grouping
// this file's own header calls load-bearing was written twice with nothing holding the two together. A
// fourth kind would have broken both matches and could have been answered DIFFERENTLY in each — a
// diagnostic naming the wrong operator, which is the exact failure this mapping exists to prevent.
function divisionOperatorSpelling(op MaxonBinOp) returns String
	if op == MaxonBinOp.div 'quotient'
		return TokenKind.slash.rawValue
	end 'quotient'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.mod 'remainder'
		return TokenKind.mod.rawValue
	end 'remainder'

	panic("divisionOperatorSpelling: '{op.rawValue}' is not a division operator — its only caller feeds it `divisionOperatorForKind`'s answer, which is `div` or `mod` and panics on anything else")
end 'divisionOperatorSpelling'

// The kind a diagnostic holds → the word the author wrote, through the one classification above.
export function divisionOperatorSpellingForKind(kind CheckedDivisionKind) returns String
	return divisionOperatorSpelling(divisionOperatorForKind(kind))
end 'divisionOperatorSpellingForKind'

// The THROWING checked-division callees (A1) — the SIXTH composer behind `GtRuntime.isThrowingRuntimeCallee`.
// A checked divide exists because its divisor could be zero, and that is the whole error channel: the flag,
// the `throws` clause, the E3057 demand for a `try` all follow from an arm here answering `true`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND SINCE A1x THE FAMILY HAS A NON-THROWING MEMBER, WHICH IS WHY THIS PREDICATE IS THE HINGE OF THE
// WHOLE RUNG.** `__guarded_mod` is expanded exactly as its two siblings are, but its guard answers `0`
// rather than raising anything — `a mod -1` is total. Answer `true` for it and every `a mod -1` in the
// language becomes E3057 ("throwing division requires try") over an operation that cannot fail; answer
// `false` for a `__checked_mod` and a real divide-by-zero becomes an uncatchable `#DE`. Each arm is written
// out for that reason, and the `remainder` range STOPS at `remainder`.
export function isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return divisionKindThrows(divisionKindOf(callee))
end 'isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee'

// **THE ONE LIST OF THROWING DIVISION KINDS.** Its single CALLER is the predicate above, which is asked by
// NAME on the general call path; the list is spelled by KIND because that is the form the two exhaustive
// matches over `CheckedDivisionKind` are held to — `Parser.requireThrowingNamedTryTarget` and
// `SemanticCheck.throwingRuntimeCalleeMessage` each panic on the arm this one answers `false` for, and each
// names this function as the authority it would have come apart from. A second copy of the list is how a
// total division would come to demand a `try`, or a fallible one to lose its flag.
//
// ⚠ It had a SECOND caller — `LowerMaxonToStd.requireDivisionChannelMatchesKind`, a runtime check that the
// error-flag register a `DivisionExpansion` carried matched its kind — and that check is GONE because the
// flag now rides the variant, which makes the mismatch unrepresentable rather than caught. Nothing replaced
// it and nothing should: this comment naming a deleted function was the whole of what was left of it.
function divisionKindThrows(kind CheckedDivisionKind) returns bool
	return match kind 'k'
		quotient to remainder gives true
		guardedRemainder gives false
		notCheckedDivision gives false
	end 'k'
end 'divisionKindThrows'

// What a checked divide throws — this family's speller, beside the four in `ManagedFileRuntime`,
// `ManagedDirectoryRuntime` and `ManagedMemoryRuntime`, and read through the one `GtRuntime.runtimeThrowsClause`.
export function divisionByZeroThrows() returns ThrowsClause
	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(DivisionByZeroErrorTypeName))
end 'divisionByZeroThrows'

// ⭐ **THE ONE CASE, AND THE FLAG DERIVED FROM IT.** `__DivisionByZeroError` has exactly one case, so its
// ordinal is 0 and its error flag is `0 + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias` — which is 1, which is exactly what an
// `x == 0` compare already produces. That coincidence is what makes the expansion branchless: the divisor
// test IS the error flag, with no `select` and no second constant. It is also FRAGILE in the one way a
// derived value can be, so `emitDivisorZeroFlag` asserts it rather than assuming it (see there).
let DivisionByZeroOrdinal = 0 as ParsedInt
export let DivisionByZeroFlag = DivisionByZeroOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias

// The `const` payload the divisor is tested against, in BOTH domains: the integer 0 and the IEEE-754 pattern
// for `+0.0` are the same 64 bits, so one constant serves the integer and the float arm of the expansion and
// neither has to spell a bare literal. (`-0.0` is a DIFFERENT pattern and is caught anyway — the float arm
// compares as f64, and IEEE-754 makes the two zeros equal.)
export let DivisorZeroPattern = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE OTHER DIVISOR `idiv` CANNOT SURVIVE (A1x)** — the divisor at which the quotient overflows, which
// is `-1` and only `-1`: `i64.min / -1` is `i64.max + 1`. It is ONE constant read by two tiers, which is the
// point of it living here rather than at either — the parser's PROOF (`TypeRules.rangeExcludesNegativeOne`,
// asking whether a divisor's declared range can hold this value) and the lowering's EMISSION (the `const`
// the remainder guard compares against, and reuses as its decrement) must be the same number or the guard
// tests one thing and elides on another.
//
// ⚠ It is the same 64 bits as `TypeRules.UnsignedFullUpperPattern` and is NOT that constant. That one means
// "the `u64.max` bound as it rides in a signed field"; this one means "the divisor that overflows". Two
// facts, one bit pattern — and `rangeExcludesNegativeOne` has to tell them apart to be correct at all.
export let OverflowingDivisorPattern = -1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE ORDINAL DRIFT GUARD (7b), FOR THE FIFTH FAMILY.** The enum's case ORDER is this family's wire
// format and `DivisionByZeroOrdinal` above is a transcription of it, so the two are asserted to agree on
// every compile — exactly as the four `install*Runtime` guards do, and for the same reason: drift is
// otherwise SILENT, because every `otherwise (e)` arm would simply name the wrong case.
//
// ONE case is not an excuse to skip the claim; it is the cheapest possible claim, and a family that does not
// make one reintroduces precisely the drift 7b closed. It is a free function rather than the tail of an
// `install…` because this family installs nothing — the caller is `Compiler.maxon`, beside the four installs
// whose own claims run there transitively.
export function verifyDivisionByZeroOrdinals(project Project)
	// `overSynthesized`, and it is the honest word rather than the old neutral `create`: `__DivisionByZeroError`
	// is declared in no stdlib file at all, so the compiler still builds this layout and only the compiler's
	// own source can make it disagree — which is what keeps this family's verdict a `panic` (A1s).
	// ⚠ **THE INSTALLER IS `lowerDivisionExpansion`, AND THIS STRING IS A CLAIM NOTHING CHECKS.** A1x
	// renamed it from `lowerCheckedDivision` when the TOTAL remainder expansion joined the fallible ones,
	// so the name A1s's rebase carried named a function that no longer exists — a dead claim in a string,
	// which is exactly the shape `error-codes check` exists to refuse for codes. Integrated by hand.
	var divisionErrors = BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck.overSynthesized(project, declaration: builtinDivisionByZeroErrorEnum(), installer: b"lowerDivisionExpansion")
	divisionErrors.claim(DivisionByZeroCaseName, ordinal: DivisionByZeroOrdinal)
	// ONE case, and the totality arm is still owed a call for the same reason the claim was (W135): the day a
	// second case is added to the layout, this is what refuses it rather than exempting it by omission. Over a
	// `synthesized` layout that refusal is a PANIC — only this compiler's own source can add the case.
	divisionErrors.requireTotal()
end 'verifyDivisionByZeroOrdinals'
